        "predicate": "summary",
        "object": "<p>The system of ocean current that moves heat in the Atlantic Ocean plays a key role in regulating climate. Today’s monitoring of it may be discontinued</p><p>Imagine we detect a large asteroid heading straight for Earth. We are able to intervene and prevent disaster, but instead we cut the funding needed to track it. A few million dollars, it was argued, was too expensive to have a chance to save society.</p><p>While this scenario isn’t real, the metaphor is alarmingly accurate. In Europe, we spend €1bn to monitor space for asteroids, even if the actual risk of a civilisation-ending asteroid strike is <a href=\"https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/asteroids/facts/#:~:text=It%20is%20highly%20unlikely%20that%20an%20asteroid,NASA's%20Jet%20Propulsion%20Laboratory%20in%20Southern%20California.\">close to zero</a>.</p> <a href=\"https://www.theguardian.com/commentisfree/2026/jun/14/amoc-collapse-europe-climate\">Continue reading...</a>"
